Also `dedup_by` closure precedence: `a.link == b.link && !a.link.is_empty() || a.title == b.title` → `(&&) before (||)` → fine.

Also fetch sorts *after* merge across feeds; items from one feed.

Additionally in `unreachable_feed_reports_failure_and_keeps_cache`: fetch returns ModuleReply::err → `.ok == false` ✓ and text contains "failed:"? My fetch_all returns Err(format!("...")) with failures joined — the message for a failed fetch is `"fetch http://...: <err>"` only if... wait: when ALL feeds fail, `fetched == 0`, `merged` empty → msg = "0/1 feed(s) updated, 0 headline(s)\nfailed:\n  fetch http://..." — but then I `return Ok(msg)`? Let me check my code: fetch_all builds msg and returns Ok(msg) always — never Err! So `assert!(!report.ok)` fails.

Hmm: `fetch_all` signature Result<String,String>; at the end it returns Ok(msg) with failures listed. The test expects err. Which behavior is better? Showing partial failures as success text with a `failed:` section is fine, but for `execute` semantics: if nothing succeeded, an error reply (red "!") is more visible. Let's make fetch_all return Err when `fetched == 0`, else Ok(msg) (with failures appended if any).

Then the unreachable test: `!report.ok` ✓ and text contains "failed:" — but if we return Err(msg) the msg includes failures ✓ (include the failure list in the error string).

